# Square Root of n (with fraction) using Binary Search

Binary search on the answer over real numbers — narrow down [lo, hi] until
the range is smaller than desired precision.

## Fixed iteration count version (avoids precision-loop edge cases)

    double sqrtBinarySearch(double n, int iterations = 100) {
        double lo = 0, hi = max(1.0, n);
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            double mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
            if (mid * mid <= n) lo = mid;
            else hi = mid;
        }
        return lo;
    }
100 iterations gives far more precision than double can even represent (converges way past 1e-15), so it's a safe default.

Time complexity: O(log(range/precision)) ≈ O(log(1e18)) ≈ ~60 iterations for 1e-9 precision, or fixed O(iterations)
N limit: n up to ~1e18 works fine (double precision starts losing accuracy above ~1e15-1e16 though — use long double for very large/high-precision needs)

# Integer Square Root of n using Binary Search

    long long sqrtBinarySearch(long long n) {
        long long lo = 0, hi = n;
        while (lo < hi) {
            long long mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;   // upper mid, avoids infinite loop
            if (mid * mid <= n) lo = mid;
            else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return lo; // largest integer whose square <= n
    }

## Why upper mid (+1)
Since we move lo = mid when condition holds, using lower mid can get stuck
in an infinite loop (lo never advances past hi-1 in some cases). Upper mid
avoids this.

## Watch out for overflow
mid * mid can overflow int for n close to 1e18 — always use long long, and
if n itself can be up to 1e18, check mid > n / mid instead of mid*mid <= n
to avoid overflow entirely:

    long long sqrtBinarySearchSafe(long long n) {
        long long lo = 0, hi = n;
        while (lo < hi) {
            long long mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2; // long long mid = (l + r) >> 1;
            if (mid <= n / mid) lo = mid;   // equivalent to mid*mid <= n, overflow-safe
            else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }

Time complexity: O(log n)
N limit: n up to ~1e18 safely with the overflow-safe version
